# Sim lifecycle (agent-run)

After any sim or eval **this agent started**, stop the stack before ending
the turn. Failures are already on disk under `docs/status/evidence/<run>/`
(`dumps/`, `ledger.jsonl`, `suite.json`, `final_layout/`). Do not leave
Gazebo / RViz / MoveIt occupying CPU or GPU for post-mortem.

## Must

- Agent evals: `gui:=false use_rviz:=false` and a dedicated `ROS_DOMAIN_ID`
  (convention: `7`). Write the launch PID to
  `/tmp/elfin_humble_sim.pid` (or `$ELFIN_SIM_PIDFILE`).
- When the driver exits, run `scripts/stop_sim.sh`. That sends SIGINT to
  `ros2 launch` (same as Ctrl+C), waits, then SIGTERM leftover PIDs.
- Do not `pkill -f "ign gazebo"` (matches the calling shell). Do not
  SIGKILL `rviz2` / `move_group` as the first step.
- Do not keep a GUI sim up so a human can "see what happened". Open the
  dump directory instead.

## One sim at a time (queue for Gazebo)

The Gazebo stack is an exclusive machine resource. Two worlds contend for the
GPU, physics stepping, and `/clock`, so both runs produce untrustworthy timing
and evidence.

- Before launching, check for an existing stack:
  `pgrep -af 'ros2 launch luggage_gazebo'` and `/tmp/elfin_humble_sim.pid`
  (or `$ELFIN_SIM_PIDFILE`). If one is up, queue: wait for it to finish, or
  ask the user. Never start a second world alongside it.
- A separate `ROS_DOMAIN_ID` isolates the ROS graph, not GPU/CPU contention.
  It is not permission to run a parallel sim.
- Release the slot as soon as the driver exits: run `scripts/stop_sim.sh`
  before writing notes or evidence, not after.
- When several agents need sim time, serialize through the `discuss/` thread
  that owns the run; do not race on `OPEN.md` rows.

## Clean-room gate before every experiment

A stopped launch is not proof that the machine is clean. A visualization,
replay, or prior eval can leave `clock_bridge` / `camera_bridge`
(`ros_gz_bridge/parameter_bridge`) reparented to systemd. These processes are
outside the launch tree and are not currently matched by `stop_sim.sh`. They
can survive into the next run, publish a second `/clock`, double the camera
traffic, and starve GPU inference without any model or weight failure.

Before **each** scored run or streak slot:

1. Stop the preceding test/replay driver, then run `scripts/stop_sim.sh` for
   its launch tree. Do this between consecutive runs, not only at the end of
   the streak.
2. Inspect for residual sim and bridge processes with a bounded command such
   as
   `pgrep -af 'ros2 launch luggage_gazebo|ign gazebo|gz sim|ros_gz_bridge|parameter_bridge|clock_bridge|camera_bridge'`.
   A process reparented to PID 1/systemd is still a live residual. Gracefully
   terminate only processes proven to belong to the completed repo eval; do
   not kill a user's or another agent's stack.
3. Before the next launch, require zero prior sim `/clock` publishers and no
   prior eval process using GPU compute. After launch, require exactly one
   `/clock` publisher. Also sanity-check the expected camera and inference
   rates before accepting timing or performance evidence.
4. If there are two `/clock` publishers, unexpectedly doubled camera rate, or
   collapsed inference rate, stop and clean the stack. Mark that run
   contaminated; do not diagnose weights, tune thresholds, or count it toward
   a consecutive-pass gate.

PF-R10 reference signature: orphan bridges produced two `/clock` publishers,
about 50 Hz camera traffic, and about 1 Hz YOLO. With exclusive teardown the
same workload held one `/clock` publisher and about 29 Hz YOLO, showing that
the earlier slowdown was resource contention rather than lost weights.

## Rehearse startup before spending a scored slot

A launch that never reaches a ready stack has not tested the product. Treat
startup as its own deterministic subsystem with its own loop.

- Use `scripts/sim_startup_rehearsal.sh` to repeat launch, staged readiness and
 teardown without scoring anything. It writes no campaign verdict, so it is
 not bound by a one-scored-run budget.
- Observe every stage with one persistent probe under one clock, measuring each
 stage's deadline from the moment its predecessor completed. Two probes on two
 clocks produce deadlines that expire against work the gate does not own.
- Do not poll readiness with `ros2 param get` / `ros2 node list` in a loop.
 Each call builds and destroys a node, costs seconds, and adds discovery churn
 to the window a startup race is already losing in.
- The installed `gz_ros2_control` 0.7.20 fetches the model URDF with one
 parameter request and waits indefinitely for that future. One of twenty
 measured launches left the future incomplete; the transport/executor cause
 below that boundary was not isolated. The signature is
 `connected to service!!` with neither `Received URDF from param server` nor
 the plugin's own retry line. The launch aborts this as
 `startup_failed: plugin_urdf_not_received` within
 `startup_watchdog_sec`; relaunch, and do not rebuild the ordering again.
 Evidence: `docs/status/evidence/platform_free_height/2026-09-16_startup_rehearsal/`.
- Pre-scoring startup attempts are bounded by wall clock, not by a fixed
 restart count, provided zero cases were attempted, hashes are unchanged, T2
 for the failed startup is complete, and residuals are zero. After the first
 scored case starts, no restart is permitted.

## Preserve failed cases before teardown

Teardown must not erase the only useful copy of a failure. Follow the shared
tiered capture and perception/planning boundary rules in
`.cursor/rules/debug-evidence.mdc`. Every scored sim plan must define its dump
matrix before launch. On failure, freeze and flush the required trigger window
under `docs/status/evidence/<topic>/<run>/` before stopping or replacing the
stack.

If only the final metric or `summary.json` was captured, mark the failure as
incomplete evidence. Add the missing trigger/ring-buffer capture before
rerunning the same gate; repeated uninstrumented failures waste the exclusive
sim slot and are not acceptable diagnosis work.

## Do not

- Stop a sim the user launched and is still using.
- Stop another agent's running sim to take the slot.
- Run `--all` unless leftover dual-sim is the problem.
